## 2.1 Requirement hierarchy

When implementing and testing the application, follow this hierarchy:

1. Approved Master PRD.
2. Approved supporting technical specifications.
3. Explicitly approved product-owner decisions and change records.
4. Behaviour of functionality built from Product P1 onward, and compatibility requirements for it.
5. This testing and production-readiness specification.

This document does not independently authorize new product features or changes to approved product behavior.

Where specifications conflict, the AI coding agent must document the conflict and request a decision rather than silently choosing one.

## 2.2 Unresolved requirements

The agent must not invent values for unresolved requirements.

Examples include:

- Post character limits.
- Poll option limits.
- Location discovery radius.
- Game scoring and difficulty rules.
- Trending calculation coefficients.
- Data retention periods.
- Account deletion grace periods.
- Performance targets not yet approved.
- Traffic and concurrency expectations.
- Recovery Point Objective (RPO).
- Recovery Time Objective (RTO).

For each unresolved requirement:

1. Identify the relevant document and requirement.
2. Explain why the decision affects implementation or testing.
3. Propose a reasonable option where useful.
4. Explain the technical, security and operational implications.
5. Obtain approval before treating the proposal as an approved requirement.

The agent may create provisional test cases using explicitly labelled assumptions, but must not represent assumed values as approved product requirements.

These values remain TBD (owner: Dennis) — see the consolidated Open Decisions register. Do not invent them.

# 3. NON-NEGOTIABLE PRODUCTION PRESERVATION RULES

Once Product P1 work exists, Kea Parrot will contain working features, user accounts, production data, database structures, integrations and operational configurations. No prototype exists yet (confirmed 2026-11-20), so in Readiness R0 there is nothing to preserve.

Testing and production-readiness work must preserve them from Product P1 / Readiness R1 onward.

## 3.1 Foundation-first approach (no existing system)

There is no existing system to inspect: no prototype exists (confirmed 2026-11-20). Before changing anything built from Product P1 onward, the agent MUST inspect that system.

The inspection of work built from Product P1 onward must cover:

- Repository structure.
- Current application framework and version.
- Package dependencies.
- Build and deployment configuration.
- Routes and application modules.
- Authentication implementation.
- Authorization and role management.
- Database schema and relationships.
- Migration history.
- RLS policies.
- Database functions and triggers.
- Storage buckets and access policies.
- API routes and integrations.
- Realtime subscriptions and event handling.
- Environment variables and secrets management.
- Automated tests.
- Monitoring and logging.
- Production deployment configuration.
- User-facing workflows.

The agent must not assume that any implementation matches the target architecture exactly.

## 3.2 Prohibited operations without explicit approval

These prohibitions apply to all work from Product P1 / Readiness R1 onward. In Readiness R0 there is no production system for them to act on.

The agent MUST NOT:

- Reset the production database.
- Drop production tables to recreate them.
- Delete existing user records.
- Delete existing administrator records.
- Replace authentication tables.
- Change existing primary-key types without an approved migration plan.
- Change production user identifiers.
- Remove existing database relationships without approval.
- Disable RLS to make application requests work.
- Replace working authentication with a new implementation without approval.
- Remove existing API routes without compatibility analysis.
- Delete existing storage objects or buckets.
- Overwrite production environment variables without validation.
- Remove working integrations because they are not part of a new specification.
- Run destructive test suites against production.
- Introduce test data into production without an approved cleanup strategy.
- Expose service-role credentials or privileged secrets to the browser.
- Hide failing tests by weakening assertions or skipping tests.
- Modify security controls simply to make automated tests pass.

## 3.3 Database migration safety

From Product P1 onward, every database change must follow a controlled migration process. In Readiness R0 the first migration creates the schema; there is no current production schema to inspect.

Required steps:

1. Inspect the current schema (from Product P1 onward; none exists in Readiness R0).
2. Inspect migration history.
3. Compare the current schema with the approved target design.
4. Identify affected tables, columns, indexes, policies, triggers and functions.
5. Identify possible data loss or compatibility issues.
6. Prepare a migration plan.
7. Test the migration against a representative non-production database.
8. Verify existing records remain intact.
9. Verify application compatibility.
10. Obtain approval for risky production changes.
11. Execute the migration through the approved deployment process.
12. Verify the resulting schema and application behavior.

Prefer backward-compatible changes.

Use expand-and-contract migrations where appropriate:

- Add compatible structures first.
- Deploy code that supports both old and new structures where necessary.
- Migrate existing data safely.
- Verify the new implementation.
- Remove obsolete structures only after explicit approval and confirmation that they are no longer required.

A successful migration must not be judged solely by the absence of SQL errors.

## 3.4 Required preservation evidence

Before and after every risky operation, maintain appropriate evidence of:

- Database schema.
- Migration version.
- Relevant record counts.
- User and administrator records (once they exist).
- Important foreign-key relationships.
- RLS policy configuration.
- Storage configuration.
- Application build and deployment version.

Do not expose sensitive personal data in test reports.

# 4. TESTING GOVERNANCE AND RESPONSIBILITIES

## 4.1 Testing principles

All testing must be:

- Requirement-driven.
- Repeatable.
- Measurable.
- Automated where practical.
- Risk-based.
- Traceable.
- Non-destructive.
- Environment-aware.
- Documented.

Every critical requirement must have one or more corresponding test cases.

Every failed test must be recorded and tracked until resolved or formally accepted as a release exception by an authorized decision-maker.

## 4.2 Testing categories

The testing framework must include:

1. Static code analysis.
2. Type checking.
3. Dependency and package validation.
4. Unit testing.
5. Integration testing.
6. Database testing.
7. RLS and authorization testing.
8. API contract testing.
9. Realtime testing.
10. UI component testing.
11. End-to-end testing.
12. Responsive testing.
13. Accessibility testing.
14. Security testing.
15. Performance testing.
16. Reliability testing.
17. Regression testing.
18. Migration testing.
19. Backup and recovery testing.
20. Deployment and rollback testing.
21. User acceptance testing.

## 4.3 Risk classification

Every defect must be assigned a severity.

| Severity | Definition | Release treatment |
|---|---|---|
| P0 — Critical | Data loss, unauthorized privileged access, widespread outage, critical security compromise | Release blocked |
| P1 — High | Major functionality unavailable, cross-user data exposure, broken authentication, serious authorization failure | Release blocked |
| P2 — Medium | Important feature degraded, significant workflow failure, limited functional defect | Normally blocks release when core functionality is affected |
| P3 — Low | Minor visual, usability or non-critical functional defect, including cosmetic inconsistency without meaningful functional impact | May be deferred with approval |

Severity scale: P0–P3 only (D-06). The former cosmetic-only severity is folded into P3.

A security vulnerability that permits unauthorized access or modification of user information must not be downgraded merely because it is difficult to reproduce.

